  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Areas of frost on Tuesday morning.\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\n-  Trending wetter late Thursday into Friday.\n\n\nAn upper-level low continues to track east toward the Northern \nPlains, leaving lingering mountain terrain showers along the \nContinental Divide. Scattered snow showers will continue for Logan\nPass in Glacier NP into this evening leading to slushy conditions\nat times. Westerly winds gusting to 20-25 mph will diminish near \nsunset, yielding a cool, autumnal afternoon.\n\nClouds will persist across northwest Montana tonight, but \nclearing skies south of Interstate 90 will create optimal \nconditions for radiational cooling by early Tuesday. Areas of \nfrost are expected in the higher valleys along the Divide and \nnorth- central Idaho, as well as cold-prone spots within the \nMissoula, Bitterroot, and Clearwater valleys. Protect sensitive \nvegetation.\n\nScattered showers remain confined near the Canadian border on \nTuesday with periodic mid- to high-level cloudiness elsewhere. \nDaytime temperatures will gradually warm back to seasonal averages\nthrough Wednesday.\n\nA wetter pattern returns Thursday into Friday as another upper-\nlevel trough slowly traverses the Northern Rockies. Deep \nsouthwesterly flow will advect monsoonal moisture into the region,\nelevating precipitable water (PWAT) values to 150-200% of normal.\nWeak bulk shear will limit organized severe weather, but diurnal \nheating will generate modest instability for scattered \nthunderstorms. Weak steering currents and high PWATs make slow- \nmoving thunderstorms containing locally heavy rainfall and \nlocalized runoff the primary hazards.\n\nEnsemble guidance agrees well (90%) on a warming and drying trend\nfrom Saturday through next Monday.",
